{"answer":"Flask ties the request and response lifecycle to a per‑request context. When a WSGI request arrives Flask creates a RequestContext (see src/flask/ctx.py). The context constructs the Request object using the app request_class, sets up a URL adapter, opens the session and pushes the context onto two contextvars: _cv_app for the application and _cv_request for the request.

The globals module (src/flask/globals.py) defines request (and session) as LocalProxy objects that read from the _cv_request ContextVar. This means any code that imports flask.request gets the current request bound to the active RequestContext. When the request ends, the context is popped, tearing down the request and invoking teardown functions.

Responses are created by the view functions: whatever a view returns is passed to Flask.make_response (in flask/app.py), which turns strings, iterables, or a Response object into a werkzeug.wrappers.Response (flask.wrappers.Response). After the view returns, the response is processed by after‑request functions registered on the RequestContext (_after_request_functions), then returned to the WSGI server. Thus Flask handles request objects via a context‑local proxy and creates response objects on demand, binding them to the same request context for the duration of the request."}

